1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,983
Four billion years ago, there was no life on Earth.

2
00:00:05,133 --> 00:00:07,150
Only water, rock… and chemistry.

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:12,517
This is a simulation of how that changed.

4
00:00:12,667 --> 00:00:18,800
We’re looking at a patch of ancient seafloor less than a millimetre wide,

5
00:00:18,950 --> 00:00:23,150
where hydrothermal vents pump out heat and raw chemicals.

6
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:27,804
Everything here is drawn at its true size.

7
00:00:27,954 --> 00:00:32,535
Zoom in tens of thousands of times, and those specks become molecules:

8
00:00:32,685 --> 00:00:37,660
nucleotides, each a phosphate, a sugar and a base, about a nanometre across.

9
00:00:37,810 --> 00:00:40,813
On warm mineral surfaces, they link into chains.

10
00:00:40,963 --> 00:00:45,150
This is RNA, the molecule that may have started it all.

11
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:50,576
Some chains fold into shapes that do something remarkable:

12
00:00:50,726 --> 00:00:55,373
they copy themselves, letter by letter, from the nucleotides around them.

13
00:00:55,523 --> 00:00:59,299
The copies aren’t perfect, so the best copiers win.

14
00:00:59,449 --> 00:01:04,097
Meanwhile, fatty molecules gather into bubbles that trap these replicators inside.

15
00:01:04,247 --> 00:01:07,150
A protocell: heredity, wrapped in a membrane.

16
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:14,322
When a protocell lineage keeps growing, dividing and copying its genes,

17
00:01:14,472 --> 00:01:19,994
it becomes something new: a living cell, a few micrometres across,

18
00:01:20,144 --> 00:01:25,150
with ribosomes building proteins, and a chromosome carrying its genes.

19
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:28,650
Now, time speeds up.

20
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:35,650
Every division copies the genome with occasional mistakes, and natural selection does the rest.

21
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:37,650
Lineages split into species.

22
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:42,650
Some learn to harvest sunlight, flooding the ocean with oxygen:

23
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:48,150
a poison to the old world, and fuel for the new.

24
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:51,150
Others learn to eat each other.

25
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:54,500
Then, a lucky accident.

26
00:01:54,650 --> 00:01:59,125
A cell swallows an oxygen-breathing bacterium… and doesn’t digest it.

27
00:01:59,275 --> 00:02:03,750
It becomes a mitochondrion, a power plant inside the cell.

28
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:11,150
This is a eukaryote: bigger, with a nucleus, and the ancestor of every plant and animal.

29
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:16,911
Some cells stop drifting apart after they divide.

30
00:02:17,061 --> 00:02:24,900
Stuck together, they share food and protection, and something new appears: a division of labour.

31
00:02:25,050 --> 00:02:28,628
Cells take on jobs: feeding, defending, moving.

32
00:02:28,778 --> 00:02:35,020
A few become a germline, budding off to start the next generation.

33
00:02:35,170 --> 00:02:37,150
Many cells… one organism.

34
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:40,772
Nothing in this story was scripted.

35
00:02:40,922 --> 00:02:45,370
Every step emerged from the one before it: chemistry, then heredity, then evolution.

36
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:49,613
This is the tree of life that grew in this one world.

37
00:02:49,763 --> 00:02:53,150
Start a new one, and history unfolds differently every time.

